B-0 (Boosty) audit r1 F2 -- tmpfile() is delete-on-close; it stops leaking
/tmp/tmpfile_XXXXXX, and it still works afterwards.

THE DEFECT THE AUDIT FOUND. Upstream tmpfile() creates the file and at once
issues a raw SYS_unlinkat, ignoring the result. That number is the 0xFFFF
sentinel, so the unlink never happened: every tmpfile() left a named file on
the persistent root for ever. Measured on the device before this patch
(2026-09-21, a probe run twice): `before=0 during=1 nlink=1 after_close=1`,
then `before=1 ... after_close=2`. 0027 fixed remove() for the same line and
missed this twin. Consumers: GNU make's os_anontmp, every port that spools.

THE DEFECT THE OBVIOUS FIX FOUND. The audit's suggestion -- call the public
unlink() instead -- was tried first, and the boot went RED in the scan leg of
pouch-hello-fopen: `fgetc=-1 feof=0 ferror=1 errno=2; raw read=-1 errno=2`. A
plain read() at EOF of the now genuinely unlinked file returned ENOENT. The
kernel is only the messenger (dev9p_read forwards the server's error; the
data bytes had been served from the Larder's own-write pages, so the FIRST
wire read was the EOF probe). The refusal is Stratum's, and it is by design:
its fid model's IOReject gate (specs/fid.tla; src/9p/server.c
verify_fresh_snapshot) rejects I/O on a fid whose file has been unlinked. On
this root filesystem an open file does NOT outlive its last name.

So "the fid survives the unlink" -- which pouch-hello-fopen's tmpfile leg
claimed to prove since 0024 -- was never true here. The leg was green because
no unlink was ever issued. One false green hid both defects.

THE FIX. The name lives exactly as long as the stream:
  - fclose() removes it, through f->close (close the fd FIRST, then unlink:
    nothing touches the fid after the name is gone);
  - streams still open at a normal exit() are swept by __stdio_exit after its
    final flushes, through a weak hook, so a program that never calls
    tmpfile() links none of this. tmpfile.o references __stdio_exit_needed
    itself (the __toread.c idiom): that symbol is otherwise pulled in only by
    __toread.o / __towrite.o, so a program using tmpfile() through fileno()
    alone would have exited through the dummy and never swept (audit r2 F5).

LOCKING (audit r2 F3 + F4 -- the first version got both wrong). tmp_lock
guards the list and an `exiting` flag and is never held across a syscall.
  - The first version held it across close + unlink: four 9P round trips,
    each a note-delivery point. A handler calling exit() there met its own
    lock in a threaded program (never exits) and, single-threaded, found the
    node already unlisted with the name still on disk (leak). Now fclose()
    closes and unlinks while the node is STILL LISTED and unlists it last, so
    an exit() landing anywhere in between still sweeps the name; the sweep
    repeating an UNLINK is harmless (ENOENT).
  - A repeated CLOSE is not harmless in a threaded program -- the number can
    have been handed to another thread in between -- so the descriptor has
    exactly one closer (audit r3 F6): whoever takes it out of the node, under
    tmp_lock, closes it. That is fclose(), the __fdopen-failure arm, or the
    sweep; never two. (Only the failure arm could actually overlap the sweep:
    fclose() holds the FILE lock across f->close and __stdio_exit takes every
    listed FILE's lock before it sweeps. The rule is uniform anyway.)
  - The first version's sweep took the lock and kept it, so a thread that
    had just open()ed its file blocked on the lock for ever with a name the
    sweep never saw. Now the sweep sets `exiting`, releases the lock, and
    walks a list that is frozen from then on: fclose() leaves its node in
    place, and tmpfile() removes its own new file instead of listing it.
  - tmp_lock is always innermost, and every hold is a few pointer stores --
    the window upstream's open-file-list lock has always had.
This is best effort by nature, and the first version's "an exit racing either
call cannot miss a name that is on disk" was false. A process that dies
without running exit() (_exit, abort, a kill) leaves its names behind, and so
does an exit() -- a note handler's, or another thread's reaching _Exit first --
that lands between open() returning and the node being listed. POSIX calls
that case implementation-defined; closing it needs a boot-time /tmp sweep,
not libc, and is recorded as owed.

COUPLINGS this creates (audit r2 F8; r3 F5 found the one that is LIVE):
  - posix_spawn file actions -- LIVE TODAY. posix_spawn resolves a
    `dup2(fileno(t), 0)` action into the child's fd list: the child gets the
    SAME fid. `t = tmpfile(); fill; rewind; posix_spawn(child, stdin =
    fileno(t)); fclose(t)` unlinks while the child holds it, and the child's
    first uncached read meets Stratum's IOReject. It worked before this patch
    only because nothing was ever unlinked. No in-tree consumer; a port that
    hands a tmpfile() to a child must keep the stream open until the child is
    reaped.
  - fork: safe today only because _Fork's SYS_clone is a sentinel. A child
    would inherit the list, and its exit() would unlink the PARENT's live
    files -- which on this filesystem kills the parent's streams. Wiring fork
    needs a child-side reset of tmp_head (and tmp_lock in the atfork set).
  - dup: GNU make's os_anontmp is tmpfile() -> dup(fileno) -> fclose. dup()
    is ENOSYS today, so make falls back. The day dup() works, that fclose
    deletes the file under the descriptor make keeps.
  - freopen(): replaces f->close with the new stream's, orphaning the node
    (the name is then swept at exit, not at fclose). freopen() cannot succeed
    today -- it needs dup3 -- so this is latent behind the same seam as dup.
  - the name is resolved again at fclose(): a Proc that pivots or binds over
    /tmp in between unlinks in the wrong /tmp or leaks.
  - tmpfile() and the sweep call the interposable unlink() (as 0023 already
    does with open()), where upstream used raw syscalls.

NOT FIXED HERE, and bigger than tmpfile(): every POSIX program that unlinks a
file it still has open -- the classic private-scratch idiom, which is exactly
what upstream tmpfile() is -- loses the file on this system. That is a
filesystem-semantics question (Stratum's IOReject vs POSIX's "removal is
postponed until all references are closed"), raised with the operator as its
own item. Stratum's O_TMPFILE-shaped anonymous inodes (stm_fs_create_anon) are
the natural future substrate for tmpfile(); the kernel has no create flag for
them today, and adding one is a syscall-interface change.

Device-side pin (same commit, not in this patch file): pouch-hello-fopen's
tmpfile leg writes three pages, reads them back to a clean EOF through the
wire, fclose()s, and requires the count of /tmp/tmpfile_* names to be what it
was before; a self-respawned child then leaves a tmpfile OPEN at exit() and
the parent requires the count unchanged again.

